\begin{abstract}
We present the Scientific Compressed Cosmos Vortex (SCCV--1) platform, a unified
computational architecture integrating Kerr--Cartan geodesic physics, post-quantum
telemetry pipelines, and tensor-weighted adaptive mesh dynamics within a real-time
GPU execution environment. The theoretical foundation models differential spacetime
manifolds with spin-torsion ($\mathbf{Man}_{\mathrm{EC}}$) as a covariant functor
mapping directly into parallel CUDA algebraic structures ($\mathbf{Alg}_{\mathrm{CUDA}}$).

To suppress long-horizon numerical drift in high-spin Kerr--Cartan spacetimes
near the outer horizon ($r \rightarrow r_{+}$), we construct a fourth-order
Runge--Kutta (RK4) geodesic integrator coupled to a non-linear Gauss--Newton
constraint projection operator. This projection enforces machine-precision
preservation of the Hamiltonian norm ($\mathcal{H}$), axial angular momentum
($L_{z}$), and Carter’s constant ($\mathcal{Q}$), yielding bounded residuals
$\Delta\mathcal{H}/\mathcal{H}_{0} \leq 1.08\times10^{-15}$ over $10^{7+}$ integration
steps.

Spatial discretization is governed by a tensor-weighted Adaptive Mesh Refinement
(AMR) engine evaluating a composite curvature scalar
